1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,026
Why do you lurch forward when a bus suddenly brakes?

2
00:00:06,396 --> 00:00:08,954
Nobody pushed you. So what just happened?

3
00:00:09,694 --> 00:00:13,220
The answer hides in three rules from Sir Isaac Newton.

4
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:17,418
Let's master them with autos, cricket balls and an ISRO rocket!

5
00:00:18,968 --> 00:00:21,526
Law number one. The law of inertia.

6
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:26,950
An object stays at rest, or keeps moving in a straight line at constant speed,

7
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:29,573
unless an unbalanced force acts on it.

8
00:00:29,923 --> 00:00:33,217
Balanced forces cancel, like a tug of war between equal teams.

9
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:35,384
Unbalanced forces change the motion.

10
00:00:36,384 --> 00:00:39,483
Back to the bus. Your body was moving with it.

11
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:43,781
The bus stops, but your body keeps going. So you lurch forward!

12
00:00:45,211 --> 00:00:49,059
That is inertia: the tendency to resist any change in motion.

13
00:00:49,409 --> 00:00:53,717
Inertia of rest. Yank a tablecloth fast and the plates stay put.

14
00:00:53,667 --> 00:00:56,750
Flick the card and the coin drops into the glass.

15
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,709
Beat a dusty carpet and the dust falls out.

16
00:01:02,139 --> 00:01:06,955
Inertia of motion. A runner keeps running a few steps after the finish line.

17
00:01:07,825 --> 00:01:11,674
Inertia of direction. When an auto turns left, you slide right.

18
00:01:13,144 --> 00:01:16,025
Heavier objects have more inertia. Mass measures inertia.

19
00:01:17,025 --> 00:01:21,517
That is why seatbelts matter. In a crash the car stops, but you don't.

20
00:01:21,467 --> 00:01:23,454
The belt stops you safely.

21
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:30,600
Which is harder to push: an empty cart, or a cart full of vegetables?

22
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:35,283
The full one! Now give a cricket ball and a football the same kick.

23
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:38,024
The lighter cricket ball speeds up more.

24
00:01:39,814 --> 00:01:43,583
Law number two. Acceleration is directly proportional to the net force, and

25
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:45,598
inversely proportional to the mass.

26
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:49,328
Together, that is F equals m times a.

27
00:01:50,198 --> 00:01:53,217
F is force in newtons. m is mass in kilograms.

28
00:01:53,167 --> 00:01:56,305
a is acceleration in metres per second squared.

29
00:01:57,175 --> 00:02:01,283
Momentum is mass times velocity. Force is how fast momentum changes.

30
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:04,249
For constant mass, that is F equals m times a.

31
00:02:04,599 --> 00:02:08,770
Double the force, double the acceleration. Double the mass, half the acceleration.

32
00:02:10,170 --> 00:02:14,450
Numerical time! An auto-rickshaw of five hundred kilograms gets a net force of one

33
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:19,180
thousand newtons. Find its acceleration, and its speed after five seconds from rest.

34
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:25,396
Given: m is five hundred kilograms, F is one thousand newtons, u is zero.

35
00:02:26,136 --> 00:02:31,350
a equals F divided by m, so a equals one thousand divided by five hundred, which is

36
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:33,533
two metres per second squared.

37
00:02:34,663 --> 00:02:40,483
Then v equals u plus a times t, which is zero plus two times five, so ten metres per

38
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:41,414
second.

39
00:02:43,304 --> 00:02:45,862
That is thirty six kilometres per hour!

40
00:02:48,192 --> 00:02:50,428
Law number three! Action and reaction.

41
00:02:51,078 --> 00:02:54,604
For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.

42
00:02:54,954 --> 00:03:00,092
If A pushes B, then B pushes A back, equally hard, in the opposite direction.

43
00:03:00,442 --> 00:03:05,258
An ISRO rocket pushes hot gases down, and the gases push the rocket up.

44
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:11,209
A swimmer pushes water back, and the water pushes the swimmer forward.

45
00:03:12,079 --> 00:03:16,283
A gun pushes the bullet forward, and the bullet kicks the gun back.

46
00:03:16,233 --> 00:03:17,541
That is recoil.

47
00:03:18,671 --> 00:03:23,809
When you walk, your foot pushes the ground back, and the ground pushes you forward.

48
00:03:24,809 --> 00:03:29,303
A released balloon: air rushes out, and the balloon zooms the other way!

49
00:03:30,933 --> 00:03:35,104
But if the forces are equal and opposite, why don't they cancel?

50
00:03:35,454 --> 00:03:40,150
Because they act on different objects! Action on the ball, reaction on your foot.

51
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:42,528
Forces cancel only on the same object.

52
00:03:44,858 --> 00:03:47,739
Recap! Law one: objects resist changes in motion.

53
00:03:48,479 --> 00:03:51,037
Law two: force equals mass times acceleration.

54
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:54,913
Law three: every action has an equal and opposite reaction.

55
00:03:55,663 --> 00:03:58,221
Quick quiz! Three questions, five seconds each.

56
00:03:59,391 --> 00:04:02,583
Question one. A bus stops and you lurch forward.

57
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:05,497
A, law one? B, law two? C, law three?

58
00:04:11,247 --> 00:04:12,515
A! Law one.

59
00:04:13,685 --> 00:04:17,117
Question two. Twenty newtons acts on a four kilogram trolley.

60
00:04:17,067 --> 00:04:19,950
What is its acceleration in metres per second squared?

61
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,049
A, two. B, five. C, eighty.

62
00:04:27,799 --> 00:04:30,357
B! Twenty divided by four is five.

63
00:04:31,657 --> 00:04:35,383
Question three. A rocket rises because gases are pushed down.

64
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:38,086
A, law one? B, law two? C, law three?

65
00:04:43,836 --> 00:04:45,427
C! Action and reaction.

66
00:04:46,697 --> 00:04:49,583
You nailed it! Go spot Newton's laws everywhere.

67
00:04:49,533 --> 00:04:50,868
See you next time!
